#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f0f39f69-9d92-56bb-90ac-b8dc7ba8cff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4254" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\nlim_(x → - ∞) - 3(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} =-3 \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{2})^{x}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1717/Source/Images/5846ea3956bb36f7b5955be3d8294c64.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b0ab103e-1521-507e-b69b-5fd5256cf051" :question/math-academy-id "e-45" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1)$." :question/worked-solution "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)(3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1) &= 3^{2(1)} + 2^{3(1)} + 1 \\\\\n&= 9 + 8 + 1 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "bfe24c48-c5eb-59ca-b34d-7e0540079c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-46" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}$." :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞) - 5(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} &= -5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1717/Source/Images/5a86ab6087c88635878b30b98ffed525.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e725ca-fd00-51ab-9408-80b5ef500b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e725ca-fd00-51ab-9408-80b5ef500b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250958} :answer-field/id #uuid "6371c250-6d55-58ba-acec-66d4d7eb2325" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250957}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3419a6d-4719-5900-9c80-808b1ab34f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-302435" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/02d46e13c84fc575f489a9870f3b1213eb3d180f3f5ef6e6a546d3acfe4b2282.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound  as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=2\\cdot \\infty =\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd15216-9241-5437-87a5-7aa85fdcab4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd15216-9241-5437-87a5-7aa85fdcab4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250961} :answer-field/id #uuid "80c5c700-664e-540f-8ef2-d94ec6e8a84c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250960}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3510d7de-32fd-5111-93cc-455f85f59c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-339038" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1}=4,$ what is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can evaluate this limit in terms of the constant $c$ using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{5}^{x}+c}{{2}^{x}-1} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}({5}^{x}+c)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}({2}^{x}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{1}+c}{{2}^{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+c}{1} \\\\  & =5+c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the above limit is equal to $4,$ we can equate the above with $4,$ and then solve for $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+c & =4 \\\\ c & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee2e521-51c4-542b-9048-fa9e3fdecd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250964} {:answer/id #uuid "1daa7f40-2c49-5fa3-8437-18dfb24ce1f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1daa7f40-2c49-5fa3-8437-18dfb24ce1f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250965} :answer-field/id #uuid "8394ae01-9999-5011-a442-3e3b60b55055" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250963}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a67fb5a2-d339-56cf-b965-422c99215cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339068" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{4}^{x/2} & ={4}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b22c97df-d833-5ca1-8878-d4d3b24e6516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22c97df-d833-5ca1-8878-d4d3b24e6516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250968} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dcc540-a54a-5f42-85ad-e32a190fce5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250967}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c1511ad-2957-5cf6-967e-dc706d69b890" :question/math-academy-id "q-339070" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({3}^{x}+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({2}^{x}-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+5}{{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+5}{4-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{1} \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0556e7bb-aa73-5cc1-b118-289ddc1ea41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250971} {:answer/id #uuid "393f08f1-3c62-5ef1-ba0c-b21b5ad7ed40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "393f08f1-3c62-5ef1-ba0c-b21b5ad7ed40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250972} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a93e47a-a814-5dff-ae9d-c84009a5b465" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250970}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdb890f6-825b-5339-a7fb-0c9b108e7781" :question/math-academy-id "q-339406" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x} & =(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/9a249437f97a441c409b17e0f30a59bdcb70df02610f18d9320361d3d0d6708a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fba77c8-3557-5881-95d4-d8c0513fe4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250975} {:answer/id #uuid "9d98f1d6-553f-584d-ac4f-7ba5e172292a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d98f1d6-553f-584d-ac4f-7ba5e172292a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250976} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c582a4-b176-5b24-8b2a-5acf8646191b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250974}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d10630fd-3a94-50f6-b43a-e939590b1c90" :question/math-academy-id "q-339417" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x}) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{-3})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/60c4ef99a2617fd076e6350ba68e14311ebc41b9b0f33fcc07b871198dfc103d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88d55ed5-4e97-50b9-9ee2-941ef114b225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250979} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b33a7e-3f62-549